# Authoring mGear Shifter components

Reference source (read-only, never edit): `C:\Users\andre\Documents\GitHub\mgear\release\scripts\mgear`.
Closest stock template for our components: `shifter_classic_components/hydraulic_01`.

## How Shifter finds a component

- `MGEAR_SHIFTER_COMPONENT_PATH` must point at the **parent** folder (`components/`),
  not at the component folder. Multiple paths are `os.pathsep`-separated.
- Every direct subfolder of that path is treated as a component and must contain `__init__.py`
  (with `class Component(component.Main)`) and `guide.py` (with `class Guide(guide.ComponentGuide)`).
- The Guide Manager lists `guide.TYPE`, so `TYPE` must equal the folder name. `icon.jpg` is unused.
- Shifter caches component dirs and modules. After adding or renaming a component call
  `mgear.shifter.clearComponentCache()` (or restart Maya) or the list will not refresh.

## Iteration loop

Full authoring workflow lives in the `mgear-shifter-component` skill. The short version: Shifter
draws guides and builds rigs under `maya.standalone`, so every change gets a real build before it
reaches the GUI.

```powershell
$env:MAYA_MODULE_PATH="C:\Users\andre\Documents\GitHub\mgear\release"
cd components
& "C:\Program Files\Autodesk\Maya2026\bin\mayapy.exe" smoke_build.py
```

In an interactive Maya session, `register.register()` re-registers the path, flushes our modules
and clears Shifter's caches. Then redraw the guide and rebuild.

Record what broke in **Lessons** below in the same change as the fix.

## API facts worth not re-deriving

- `guide.postInit` sets `save_transform` / `save_blade`; names there must match what `addObjects` creates.
- `self.settings["x"]` in the Component reads the guide param `x` added via `addParam` in `addParameters`.
- Joints: append `[transform, index]` to `self.jnt_pos`; Shifter builds the joint chain from that.
- `addAnimParam` -> keyable anim attr on the UI host. `addSetupParam` -> non-keyable TD attr.
- `transform.getTransformLookingAt(pos, lookAt, normal, axis="xy", negate=...)`: the first axis
  letter points at `lookAt`, the second aligns to `normal`.
- The blade icon is drawn in its local XY plane with the flag toward **+Y**, so
  `self.guide.blades["blade"].y` is the direction the rigger visually aimed the flag.

## Lessons

Append a dated one-liner per mistake. Never delete entries.

- 2026-09-09 The old standalone bounded-bow builder both rotated the bulge plane by `bendAngle`
  *and* scaled height by `cos(bendAngle)`. The cosine is a 2D-projection artifact of the HTML card;
  in 3D it double-counts and kills the bulge at 90 deg. Rotate the plane only.
- 2026-09-09 `angleBetween.angle` is a `doubleAngle`. Connecting it to a plain `double` makes Maya
  insert a `unitConversion`, so the downstream number is in UI units, not radians. Keep the compared
  threshold attr as `doubleAngle` too so both sides get the same conversion.
- 2026-09-09 World-space `distanceBetween` includes the rig global-control scale. Measure in the
  component root's local space (`mulMatrix` by `root.worldInverseMatrix`) so the drive is scale-safe.
- 2026-09-09 `addAnimParam` attrs do **not** land on the component's own control. They go on
  `self.uihost` (the parent component's ctl, e.g. `global_C0_ctl` for a root-parented component)
  and are prefixed with the component name: `bulge` becomes `boundedBow_bulge`. `addSetupParam`
  attrs go on the component root *inside the rig*, not the guide root. Search by attribute, never
  by a guessed node path.
- 2026-09-09 `node.createPlusMinusAverage1D` feeds constants through a `try connectAttr /
  except RuntimeError setAttr` path. Set literal constants explicitly instead of trusting it.
- 2026-09-10 "Joints keep the previous position after a rebuild" is almost never the component.
  The guide root option **Connect Joints** (on by default) makes `Rig.buildJointCache()` snapshot
  every joint name in the scene, and `_addJoint` then reuses a name match via the `keep_off=True`
  path, which moves an offset transform to the joint rather than the joint to the driver. Any joint
  that outlives its rig pins the next build to the old pose: controls look right, joints do not.
  Reproduced identically on stock `hydraulic_01`. Fix is to delete the leftover joints or turn
  Connect Joints off, not to change component code.
- 2026-09-10 `addJoint(guide_relative=...)` is only metadata for the match-guide-to-joint tool.
  It does not reposition a reused joint, so it is no help against the above.
- 2026-09-10 Before blaming our code for a build oddity, run the same scenario on the nearest stock
  component in `diag`-style throwaway script. It took one run to clear the component of a bug.
- 2026-09-11 `eulerToQuat` is the DG's sin and cos: feed `2*theta` into `inputRotateX` and
  `outputQuatX` / `outputQuatW` are `sin(theta)` / `cos(theta)`. Two trig terms from one node.
  A plain `double` connected into that `doubleAngle` gets a `unitConversion` and is read as
  **degrees**, so keep the arithmetic in degrees.
- 2026-09-11 `eulerToQuat` lives in the `quatNodes` plugin. Without `cmds.loadPlugin("quatNodes")`
  the build quietly makes `unknown` nodes instead of failing.
- 2026-09-11 `mgear.pymaya.PyNode` cannot wrap a plugin node type: `nt.getTypeClass` returns None
  and you get `TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not callable`. Keep plugin nodes as name strings;
  the `mgear.core.node.create*Node` helpers take strings as happily as attributes.
- 2026-09-11 `motionPath` output is world space. The path-driven transform needs
  `inheritsTransform` off (stock `spine_ik_01` does exactly this) and the rig scale handed back
  from the component root's world scale, or the rig scales and the component's offsets do not.
- 2026-09-11 Maya 2026 `motionPath` defaults are `worldUpType` 3 (vector), `frontAxis` 1 (Y),
  `upAxis` 2 (Z). Set all three. `worldUpType` 2 plus a `worldUpMatrix` from a transform whose
  `rotateX` is driven gives an up direction an anim attr can roll around the path.
- 2026-09-11 `setRange` clamps and has three channels, so one node covers three ramped weight
  windows. `node.createSetRangeNode` applies one `oldMin`/`oldMax` to every channel, so build it
  by hand when the channels differ.
- 2026-09-11 A guide param may share a name with an anim param that it seeds (`frequency` seeding
  `waveBillow_frequency`); they live on different nodes. But the guide root outlives the build, so
  `smoke_build._anim_plug` saw both. It now requires the component-name prefix.
- 2026-09-11 Displacing points off a spine stretches the gaps between them, worst where the
  envelope peaks, so the end bone of a chain balloons. Fix: drive a curve from the displaced points
  and respace the joints along it with `fractionMode`. Degree 1 keeps the curve running exactly
  through every point, but measured against a cubic through the same points the extra fidelity was
  nil (100% of the wave height either way at 5+ points per cycle), so it does not pay for the
  jumping tangent that comes with it.
- 2026-09-11 Equal arc length is not equal chord length: joints spaced evenly along a curve that
  kinks between them still measure unequal straight-line gaps. Assert the improvement ratio, not a
  flat result. `motionPath` `fractionMode` itself is only exact to about 1e-4 of the span.
- 2026-09-11 `applyop.gear_intmatrix_op` slerps, so decomposing its output rotation at `blend=u`
  and feeding it to a frame's up object distributes a control's roll linearly along a chain.
  Measured exactly `u * 90` from one end and `(1 - u) * 90` from the other. This is the whole
  twist-distribution mechanism in the stock spine components.
- 2026-09-11 **Never take a joint's orientation from a curve the joint's own displacement built,
  nor by aiming at the next joint.** Both flip, and the second gets worse the more joints there
  are, because the gap it measures shrinks while the cause does not. Three separate attempts and
  what each measured, sweeping phase and logging the worst frame-to-frame jump per joint:
  - Aim at the next joint, up = the wave axis: clean 178 degree flips, because the chain swings
    *through* the up axis wherever the wave is steep. An aim constraint flips when its aim and up
    line up, so pick an up the aim direction can never reach: the wave stays in its plane, so the
    plane's **normal** is always safe. Fixed those, revealing the real cause underneath.
  - A longitudinal term (here circularity, sliding points along the spine) folds the curve back on
    itself once the slide passes the gap between points. Neighbouring joints then collapse to 0.004
    apart against a 0.114 spacing, and the direction between them is noise. Worst jump tracked min
    neighbour gap, nothing else.
  - Orientation from the folded curve's own tangent (cubic + `follow`) was *worse*, 180 degrees: a
    folded curve has a real cusp, and every tangent frame reverses through it. No curve degree or
    up vector saves this.
  - What worked, 0.00 degrees at every amplitude, frequency, circularity and joint count tried:
    position from the wave curve, orientation from the **smooth spine frame underneath it** via
    `orientConstraint`. Fewer nodes than the aim constraints it replaced. Cost: the bones no longer
    point exactly down a steep wave, up to about 50 degrees off at high amplitude.
- 2026-09-11 A stability fix needs an adversarial regression test or it proves nothing, and assert
  the scenario is *actually* adversarial. "Neighbouring joints collapsed" is the wrong proxy for
  "the curve folded" (a deep fold spreads the arc and the gaps stay healthy); read the longitudinal
  offset directly and check a pair swapped order. Then sweep the controls at the same time, so a
  frame that is merely frozen cannot pass a continuity assertion by standing still.
- 2026-09-10 For any list-of-scene-objects setting, copy the stock IK Reference Array layout
  (`arm_2jnt_01/settingsUI.py`): list on the left, `<<` / `>>` stacked on the right, plus
  `InternalMove` drag reordering. A plain list with buttons underneath reads as having no
  "add selected" action at all.
